# AAIE governance

## Source of truth
- `docs/AAIE/README.md` is the single source of truth for AAIE architecture, decisions (D-register), open
  decisions (U-items) and blockers (B-register). `docs/AAIE/ENGINEERING-DECISIONS.md` holds the detail of B1–B3 and
  must stay consistent with the README.
- If code, a conversation or another document disagrees with the README, the README wins until it is changed through
  an approved update. Point out the disagreement; do not resolve it silently.

## Changing the architecture
- Any **approved** AAIE architecture change must update `docs/AAIE/README.md` in the same change: the affected
  sections and a new row in its "Version history" (version, date, what changed).
- Record every newly **approved** or **rejected** decision explicitly in the decision register, with its status.
  Never mark a decision approved without evidence of approval (who approved, when).
- Never silently add, remove, reword or change the status of decisions D1–D14. Any change to them needs explicit
  approval and a version-history entry that says what changed and why.
- Keep unresolved blockers marked **OPEN** (or "Deferred" where the README says so). A blocker may only be marked
  resolved with the recorded agreement of the module owners named in the blocker.
- Do not invent owner names. Refer to module owners by role, by the module README's owner line, or by
  `.github/CODEOWNERS`.

## Implementation
- Never begin implementing AAIE (code, contracts, migrations, prompts, endpoints, UI) on the basis of the
  architecture documentation alone. Implementation of a phase starts only after explicit approval of that phase and
  after its entry-gate blockers are resolved, as listed in the README's roadmap.
- No new agents, microservices, queues, schedulers or infrastructure for AAIE without explicit approval recorded in
  the decision register.

## Preserve existing behaviour (always)
- Do not change existing assessment workflows, candidate answer recording, speech-to-text, transcripts, stored
  answers, evaluators, proctoring, or HR evidence panels and their access as part of AAIE work.
- AAIE must never modify, override, delete, hide or replace existing assessment scores, pass/fail decisions, HR
  overrides or original evidence. AAIE only reads them through approved read paths and only writes its own data.
- An infrastructure failure must never be presented or summarised as a candidate failure.
- No combined score, weights, rankings, percentiles or hire / no-hire output; no AI integrity judgments; no expired
  group-discussion evidence — unless the decision register is changed through explicit approval.
